      "text": "Of course. Based on the provided documentation, the key components of the Gingee platform can be broken down into the following distinct areas:\n\n1.  **The Gingee Server Engine (`gingee.js`):** This is the core runtime that powers the entire platform. It handles incoming HTTP/S requests, manages the application lifecycle, and orchestrates all other components. [gingee.js]\n\n2.  **The Secure Sandbox (`gbox`):** Security is a foundational principle, and the `gbox` is its implementation. Every server script is executed within this isolated environment, preventing it from accessing the global scope or filesystem, ensuring stability and security by default. [docs/concepts.md]\n\n3.  **The Application Structure (`web` and `box` folders):** Gingee enforces a simple and predictable folder structure. All applications reside in the `/web` directory, and each app has a fundamental separation between its public assets (like `/css`, `/images`) and its private backend logic, which is securely contained within the protected `/box` folder. [docs/concepts.md, docs/app-structure.md]\n\n4.  **The `$g` Global and `gingee()` Middleware:** This is the heart of the developer experience. Every script is wrapped in the `gingee()` middleware, which provides a single, secure global object (`$g`) as the gateway for interacting with the request (`$g.request`), response (`$g.response`), logger (`$g.log`), and application configuration (`$g.app`). [docs/concepts.md, docs/server-script.md]\n\n5.  **The Flexible Routing Engine:** The platform features a powerful routing system that supports two modes which can be used together. By default, it uses zero-configuration **File-Based Routing**. For more complex needs like REST APIs, developers can opt into **Manifest-Based Routing** by creating a `routes.json` file to define dynamic URL paths with parameters. [docs/concepts.md]\n\n6.  **The Module Ecosystem:** Gingee provides a rich, \"batteries-included\" standard library of modules for common tasks like database access (`db`), file system operations (`fs`), cryptography (`crypto`), and even generating PDFs (`pdf`) and charts (`chart`). These modules are securely exposed to the sandbox environment. [docs/concepts.md, docs/features.md]\n\n7.  **Declarative Configuration:** The entire system is controlled by a series of clear manifest files. The `gingee.json` file governs the global server instance, while each application has its own `app.json` for specific settings, a `pmft.json` for declaring security permissions, and an optional `routes.json` for routing. [docs/concepts.md, docs/server-config.md, docs/app-structure.md]\n\n8.  **The Command-Line Interface (`gingee-cli`):** This is the essential all-in-one tool for developers and administrators. It handles everything from initializing new projects and scaffolding apps to performing complex lifecycle operations like packaging, installing, upgrading, and rolling back applications on a remote server. [docs/concepts.md, docs/gingee-cli.md]\n\n9.  **The Glade Admin Panel:** A built-in, privileged web application that provides a graphical user interface for all the application lifecycle management tasks available in the CLI, such as installing, upgrading, and managing permissions for all applications running on the server. [docs/glade-admin.md]",
