      "text": "Of course. Adding Angular to the comparison matrix further solidifies the strength and flexibility of the proposed SPA architecture. The core Gingee engine logic remains completely unchanged, and only the frontend-specific template and configuration values are adjusted to match the conventions of the Angular ecosystem.\n\nHere is the updated proposal, including the step-by-step workflow for an Angular developer and the completed comparison matrix.\n\n---\n\n### **Angular SPA Support on Gingee**\n\nThe developer experience for Angular follows the same seamless pattern as React and Vue.\n\n#### **1. The Developer Workflow: Step-by-Step (Angular Edition)**\n\n**Step 1: Scaffolding with `gingee-cli`**\n\nThe `add-app` command would generate a pre-configured Angular workspace.\n\n```bash\ngingee-cli add-app my-angular-spa\n```\n\nThe resulting structure would follow the conventions established by the Angular CLI:\n\n```\nweb/\n└── my-angular-spa/\n    ├── box/\n    │   ├── api/\n    │   │   └── items.js        # Sample backend API endpoint\n    │   └── app.json            # \u003c-- Configured for Angular defaults\n    ├── src/                    # \u003c-- Angular\u0027s standard source folder\n    │   ├── app/\n    │   │   ├── app.component.css\n    │   │   ├── app.component.html\n    │   │   ├── app.component.ts  # Main Angular component\n    │   │   └── app.module.ts\n    │   ├── assets/\n    │   ├── environments/\n    │   ├── main.ts             # Angular entry point\n    │   └── index.html\n    ├── .gitignore\n    ├── angular.json            # \u003c-- Angular CLI\u0027s workspace configuration\n    ├── package.json            # \u003c-- Frontend package.json with Angular dependencies\n    └── tsconfig.json           # \u003c-- TypeScript configuration\n```\n\n**Step 2: The `app.json` Configuration**\n\nThe `app.json` at `web/my-angular-spa/box/app.json` is adapted to Angular\u0027s specific defaults for the development server port and build output path.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"My Angular SPA\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"type\": \"SPA\",\n  \"spa\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"dev_server_proxy\": \"http://localhost:4200\",\n    \"build_path\": \"../dist/my-angular-spa/browser\",\n    \"fallback_path\": \"index.html\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n*   `\"dev_server_proxy\": \"http://localhost:4200\"`: This points to the default port used by the Angular CLI\u0027s `ng serve` command.\n*   `\"build_path\": \"../dist/my-angular-spa/browser\"`: This points to the default output directory for a production build created by the `ng build` command. Note that it includes the project name as a subfolder.\n\n**Step 3: The Development Experience**\n\n1.  The developer runs `npm run dev` in the Gingee project root.\n2.  The Gingee engine starts and automatically executes the `start` script within `web/my-angular-spa/package.json`, which is an alias for `ng serve`.\n3.  All traffic on `http://localhost:7070` is now handled by Gingee:\n    *   API calls like `http://localhost:7070/my-angular-spa/api/items` are processed by Gingee\u0027s backend.\n    *   All other requests are proxied to the Angular dev server on port `4200`, enabling a perfect, hot-reloading development workflow without any CORS issues.\n\n**Step 4 \u0026 5: Building and Deploying to Production**\n\nThese steps remain conceptually identical.\n\n1.  **Build:** The developer runs `npm run build` inside `web/my-angular-spa`. The Angular CLI compiles the application into static assets and places them in the `dist/my-angular-spa/browser/` directory.\n2.  **Deploy:** The production Gingee server serves the static assets from the configured `build_path` and uses the `fallback_path` (`index.html`) to enable Angular\u0027s client-side router.\n\n---\n\n#### **2. Updated SPA Support Comparison Matrix**\n\nHere is the completed matrix comparing the setup for React, Vue, and Angular. This clearly illustrates that the Gingee-side configuration is minimal and consistent, while the frontend-side specifics are handled by their respective ecosystems.\n\n| Aspect                     | React Template                                     | Vue Template                                           | Angular Template                                                 | **Gingee Impact**                               |\n| :------------------------- | :------------------------------------------------- | :----------------------------------------------------- | :--------------------------------------------------------------- | :---------------------------------------------- |\n| **Source Folder**          | `dev_src/`                                         | `src/`                                                 | `src/`                                                           | None. This is a frontend convention.            |\n| **Component Files**        | `.jsx` (e.g., `App.jsx`)                           | `.vue` (e.g., `App.vue`)                               | `.ts`, `.html`, `.css` (e.g., `app.component.ts`)                | None. Handled by the frontend build tool.       |\n| **Frontend Dependencies**  | `react`, `react-dom`, `@vitejs/plugin-react`       | `vue`, `@vitejs/plugin-vue`                            | `@angular/core`, `@angular/cli`, `typescript`                      | None. Managed by the app\u0027s own `package.json`.  |\n| **Tooling Configuration**  | `vite.config.js`                                   | `vite.config.js`                                       | `angular.json`, `tsconfig.json`                                  | None. Handled by the frontend tooling.          |\n| **Gingee `app.json`**      | `dev_server_proxy: \"http://localhost:5173\"`\u003cbr\u003e`build_path: \"../dist\"` | `dev_server_proxy: \"http://localhost:5173\"`\u003cbr\u003e`build_path: \"../dist\"` | `dev_server_proxy: \"http://localhost:4200\"`\u003cbr\u003e`build_path: \"../dist/my-angular-spa/browser\"` | **Minimal.** Only default values in the `spa` block are changed. |\n\nThis comparison confirms that the proposed SPA architecture is robust, flexible, and does not lock the developer into any specific frontend technology. The design successfully abstracts the backend\u0027s role, allowing it to support a wide range of modern frontend frameworks with only trivial configuration changes.",
