      "text": "Excellent. We are now entering **Phase 2: Gindoo Application Development**.\n\nWe will use the new SPA-aware tooling we just built to create our Kanban-style application. I will walk through the process step-by-step, starting with the backend foundation: scaffolding, database setup, and API implementation.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Scaffolding the `gindoo` Application**\n\nFirst, we use our updated `gingee-cli` to create the minimal backend structure for our SPA.\n\n**Action:** Run the `add-app` command.\n\n```bash\ngingee-cli add-app gindoo\n```\n\n**Interaction:**\n1.  At the \"What type of app is this?\" prompt, we select **`SPA`**.\n2.  When asked to configure a database, we select **`Yes`**.\n3.  We will configure a **`postgres`** database connection named `main_db`.\n4.  We will confirm **`Yes`** to generating a JWT secret.\n\n**Result:** The CLI will create the `web/gindoo/` directory containing the `box/` folder and a pre-configured `app.json`.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Defining the Database Schema and Startup Script**\n\nNext, I will define the database schema and create a startup script that Gingee will run once to automatically create the necessary tables.\n\n**Action:** Create the file `web/gindoo/box/setup/01_schema.js`.\n\n```javascript\n// File: web/gindoo/box/setup/01_schema.js\n\nmodule.exports \u003d async function() {\n    await gingee(async ($g) \u003d\u003e {\n        const db \u003d require(\u0027db\u0027);\n        const DB_NAME \u003d \u0027main_db\u0027; // The name from our app.json\n\n        $g.log.info(\u0027Running setup script for Gindoo: 01_schema.js...\u0027);\n\n        // Create the Users table\n        const createUsersTableSql \u003d `\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS \"Users\" (\n                \"id\" UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n                \"name\" VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n                \"email\" VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n                \"avatar_url\" TEXT,\n                \"created_at\" TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n            );\n        `;\n        await db.execute(DB_NAME, createUsersTableSql);\n        $g.log.info(\u0027Table \"Users\" is present.\u0027);\n\n        // Create the Tasks table with a foreign key to Users\n        const createTasksTableSql \u003d `\n            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS \"Tasks\" (\n                \"id\" UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n                \"title\" VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n                \"description\" TEXT,\n                \"status\" VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\n                \"assignee_id\" UUID REFERENCES \"Users\"(\"id\") ON DELETE SET NULL,\n                \"created_at\" TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n                \"updated_at\" TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n            );\n        `;\n        await db.execute(DB_NAME, createTasksTableSql);\n        $g.log.info(\u0027Table \"Tasks\" is present.\u0027);\n\n        $g.log.info(\u0027Gindoo database schema setup is complete.\u0027);\n    });\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Finalizing the `app.json` Configuration**\n\nNow, I will update the scaffolded `app.json` to include the `startup_scripts` property, so our schema script is executed when the application first loads.\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/box/app.json`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Gindoo\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"type\": \"SPA\",\n  \"spa\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    \"dev_server_proxy\": \"http://localhost:5173\",\n    \"build_path\": \"../dist\",\n    \"fallback_path\": \"index.html\"\n  },\n  \"db\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"postgres\",\n      \"name\": \"main_db\",\n      \"host\": \"localhost\",\n      \"user\": \"postgres\",\n      \"password\": \"your_db_password\",\n      \"database\": \"gindoo_db\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"jwt_secret\": \"a_long_generated_secret_key_from_the_cli\",\n  \"startup_scripts\": [\n    \"setup/01_schema.js\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Designing and Implementing the Backend API**\n\nWith the database ready, I will define the API routes in `routes.json` and implement the first key endpoint.\n\n**Action:** Create the file `web/gindoo/box/routes.json`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"routes\": [\n    { \"path\": \"/users\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"script\": \"api/users/list.js\" },\n    { \"path\": \"/users\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"script\": \"api/users/create.js\" },\n    { \"path\": \"/tasks\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"script\": \"api/tasks/list.js\" },\n    { \"path\": \"/tasks\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"script\": \"api/tasks/create.js\" },\n    { \"path\": \"/tasks/:taskId\", \"method\": \"PUT\", \"script\": \"api/tasks/update.js\" },\n    { \"path\": \"/tasks/:taskId\", \"method\": \"DELETE\", \"script\": \"api/tasks/delete.js\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Action:** Create the file `web/gindoo/box/api/tasks/list.js`. This is our first functional endpoint.\n\n```javascript\n// File: web/gindoo/box/api/tasks/list.js\n\nmodule.exports \u003d async function() {\n    await gingee(async ($g) \u003d\u003e {\n        const db \u003d require(\u0027db\u0027);\n        const DB_NAME \u003d \u0027main_db\u0027;\n\n        try {\n            const { assigneeId } \u003d $g.request.query;\n\n            let sql \u003d `\n                SELECT \n                    t.id, t.title, t.description, t.status, t.created_at,\n                    u.id as \"assigneeId\", u.name as \"assigneeName\", u.avatar_url as \"assigneeAvatar\"\n                FROM \"Tasks\" t\n                LEFT JOIN \"Users\" u ON t.assignee_id \u003d u.id\n            `;\n            const params \u003d [];\n\n            if (assigneeId) {\n                sql +\u003d \u0027 WHERE t.assignee_id \u003d $1\u0027;\n                params.push(assigneeId);\n            }\n\n            sql +\u003d \u0027 ORDER BY t.created_at DESC\u0027;\n\n            const tasks \u003d await db.query.many(DB_NAME, sql, params);\n\n            $g.response.send(tasks);\n\n        } catch (err) {\n            $g.log.error(\u0027Failed to fetch tasks\u0027, { error: err.message, stack: err.stack });\n            $g.response.send({ error: \u0027An internal server error occurred while fetching tasks.\u0027 }, 500);\n        }\n    });\n};\n```\n\n---\n\nThe backend foundation for the Gindoo application is now in place. We have:\n1.  A scaffolded SPA-aware backend structure.\n2.  A complete database schema that will be automatically created on startup.\n3.  A defined set of RESTful API endpoints.\n4.  A functional endpoint for listing tasks, which is ready for the frontend to consume.\n\nThe next logical step is to initialize the React frontend within the `web/gindoo` directory as instructed by the CLI and then begin building the UI components to interact with this API.\n\nShall we proceed with the frontend setup?",
