      "text": "Excellent. The backend foundation using SQLite is now solid. We will now proceed with setting up the frontend for the Gindoo application.\n\nFollowing our framework-agnostic plan, I will detail the steps a developer would take to initialize a React project using Vite inside our `web/gindoo` directory and then integrate Tailwind CSS.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 5: Initialize the Frontend Project with Vite and React**\n\nThis process uses the standard, official tooling for creating a React application.\n\n**Action:** Execute the following commands from the terminal.\n\n1.  **Navigate into the new app directory:**\n    ```bash\n    cd web/gindoo\n    ```\n\n2.  **Initialize the Vite + React project in the current directory:**\n    ```bash\n    npm create vite@latest . -- --template react\n    ```\n    *The `.` tells Vite to use the existing `gindoo` folder. The `-- --template react` arguments are passed directly to the `create-vite` script to select the React template non-interactively.*\n\n3.  **Install the frontend dependencies:**\n    ```bash\n    npm install\n    ```\n\n**Result:** The `web/gindoo` directory is now populated with the standard Vite + React project structure, including `src/`, `package.json`, `vite.config.js`, and `index.html`.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 6: Integrate Tailwind CSS**\n\nNext, we will follow the official Tailwind CSS guide for a Vite project to add utility-first styling.\n\n**Action:** Execute the following commands from within the `web/gindoo` directory.\n\n1.  **Install Tailwind CSS and its peer dependencies:**\n    ```bash\n    npm install -D tailwindcss postcss autoprefixer\n    ```\n\n2.  **Generate the configuration files:**\n    ```bash\n    npx tailwindcss init -p\n    ```\n    *This creates the necessary `tailwind.config.js` and `postcss.config.js` files.*\n\n3.  **Configure Tailwind\u0027s template paths:**\n    **File:** `web/gindoo/tailwind.config.js`\n    **Action:** Update the `content` array to tell Tailwind where our component files will be.\n\n    ```javascript\n    /** @type {import(\u0027tailwindcss\u0027).Config} */\n    export default {\n      content: [\n        \"./index.html\",\n        \"./src/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}\", // \u003c-- Update this line\n      ],\n      theme: {\n        extend: {},\n      },\n      plugins: [],\n    }\n    ```\n\n4.  **Add the Tailwind directives to the main CSS file:**\n    **File:** `web/gindoo/src/index.css`\n    **Action:** Replace the contents of this file with the Tailwind `@tailwind` directives.\n\n    ```css\n    @tailwind base;\n    @tailwind components;\n    @tailwind utilities;\n    ```\n\n---\n\n### **Step 7: Verify the Integrated Development Environment**\n\nThe entire project is now set up. The final step is to run the unified development command from the Gingee project root to ensure the backend server and the frontend proxy are working together correctly.\n\n**Action:**\n\n1.  **Navigate back to the Gingee project root:**\n    ```bash\n    cd ../..\n    ```\n\n2.  **Start the server:**\n    ```bash\n    npm run dev\n    ```\n\n**Expected Output:**\nYou will see logs from both the Gingee server starting on port 7070 and the Vite development server starting on port 5173.\n\n**Verification:**\nOpen your web browser and navigate to `http://localhost:7070/gindoo`. You will see the default React application, now styled with Tailwind CSS. More importantly, any API calls made from the React application to relative paths like `/gindoo/api/hello` will be seamlessly proxied to the Gingee backend, which you can verify in your browser\u0027s developer tools.\n\n---\n\nThe setup is complete. We have successfully:\n*   Configured a robust SQLite backend.\n*   Initialized a standard React + Vite frontend.\n*   Integrated Tailwind CSS for styling.\n*   Established a seamless, proxied development environment managed by a single command.\n\nThe project is now ready for the implementation of the specific Gindoo features, starting with the User and Task API endpoints and their corresponding React UI components.",
