      "text": "**Reviewing Error Location**\n\nI\u0027m currently reviewing the TypeScript error in `TaskModal.tsx`. It\u0027s pinpointing a missing property (`assignee_id`) within a type. My immediate focus is on understanding the data structures involved, specifically the expected properties of the object being referenced. I\u0027ll need to check the interface or type definition used by `TaskModal.tsx` and see how it aligns with the data.\n\n\n**Confirming the Typo**\n\nI\u0027ve just confirmed the cause of the TypeScript error. The code in `TaskModal.tsx` uses `assignee_id`, while the `Task` interface in `web/gindoo/src/types/index.ts` defines the property as `assigneeId` (camelCase). It\u0027s a classic typo! I\u0027ll quickly fix this discrepancy in the code to resolve the error and ensure type safety. Next step is to test the correction.\n\n\n**Addressing Data Discrepancy**\n\nHaving identified the mismatch in property names between the frontend\u0027s `Task` type (using `assigneeId`) and the backend API\u0027s expected data structure (`assignee_id`), I\u0027m now leaning towards the more robust solution: creating a separate `TaskPayload` type. This will serve as a Data Transfer Object (DTO) for the backend, allowing me to transform the frontend\u0027s `Task` state into the format the API requires. This promotes code clarity and avoids inconsistencies, a win-win.\n\n\n**Developing the DTO Strategy**\n\nI\u0027ve just finalized the plan to introduce a `TaskApiPayload` type to handle the data format required by the backend. It offers a clear separation of concerns. Now, I\u0027m defining the `TaskApiPayload` type in `types/index.ts`, mirroring the structure expected by the API. I\u0027m also preparing to update the `DataContext.tsx` functions to transform `Task` objects into this `TaskApiPayload` format before making API calls. This should enhance type safety and maintainability.\n\n\n**Defining Data Transformation**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing the plan for a `TaskApiPayload` type. The goal is a clean separation of concerns, ensuring the frontend\u0027s camelCase remains, while transforming to the backend\u0027s snake_case. This involves creating the `TaskApiPayload` type and revising the `DataContext` functions. These updates will make API calls robust and clear, without changing `TaskModal.tsx`.\n\n\n**Refining Data Transformation**\n\nI\u0027m now in the process of finalizing the `TaskApiPayload` type and the associated transformation logic within `DataContext.tsx`. It will involve mapping `Task` fields like `assigneeId` to `assignee_id` before the API call. I\u0027m also preparing tests to ensure this conversion functions correctly for different data scenarios, including the cases where `assigneeId` is `null` or `unassigned`. My goal is a robust and easily maintainable solution.\n\n\n",
