      "text": "You are absolutely right. My apologies. That is a clear and direct TypeScript error that I overlooked. Thank you for catching it—this is a perfect example of why using TypeScript is so valuable.\n\nThe error \"assignee_id does not exist in type\" occurs because our frontend `Task` interface defines the property as `assigneeId` (camelCase), but in the `TaskModal`\u0027s save handler, I was trying to create a payload object with `assignee_id` (snake_case) to match the backend API. TypeScript correctly flagged this as an error because the payload object was being checked against the `Task` interface.\n\nThe best practice is to keep our frontend code idiomatic (using camelCase) and create a separate, correctly-typed payload object specifically for the API call. This decouples our frontend data model from our backend API contract.\n\nI will now correct the implementation in all affected files.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Define the API Payload Type**\n\nFirst, let\u0027s explicitly define the shape of the data our backend API expects.\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/src/types/index.ts`.\n\n```typescript\n// File: web/gindoo/src/types/index.ts\n\nexport interface User {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  email: string;\n  avatar_url?: string;\n}\n\nexport interface Task {\n  id: string;\n  title: string;\n  description?: string;\n  status: \u0027todo\u0027 | \u0027in_progress\u0027 | \u0027done\u0027;\n  assigneeId?: string | null; // Allow null for unassigned\n  assigneeName?: string;\n  assigneeAvatar?: string;\n}\n\n// +++ NEW: Define the type for the API payload +++\nexport interface TaskApiPayload {\n    title?: string;\n    description?: string;\n    status?: \u0027todo\u0027 | \u0027in_progress\u0027 | \u0027done\u0027;\n    assignee_id?: string | null; // Use snake_case to match the backend\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Update DataContext to Handle the Transformation**\n\nThe `DataContext` is the perfect place to handle the transformation from our frontend\u0027s camelCase model to the backend\u0027s snake_case payload.\n\n**Action:** Update the `addTask` and `updateTask` functions in `web/gindoo/src/contexts/DataContext.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/contexts/DataContext.tsx\n\nimport { Task, User, TaskApiPayload } from \u0027../types\u0027; // +++ Import TaskApiPayload +++\n// ... (other imports)\n\ninterface DataContextType {\n    // ...\n    // The public-facing functions still use the clean, camelCase Partial\u003cTask\u003e\n    addTask: (taskData: Partial\u003cTask\u003e) \u003d\u003e Promise\u003cvoid\u003e;\n    updateTask: (taskId: string, updates: Partial\u003cTask\u003e) \u003d\u003e Promise\u003cvoid\u003e;\n    // ...\n}\n\nexport const DataProvider \u003d ({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) \u003d\u003e {\n    // ... (state and fetch functions)\n\n    const addTask \u003d async (taskData: Partial\u003cTask\u003e) \u003d\u003e {\n        try {\n            // +++ TRANSFORM to the API payload format +++\n            const payload: TaskApiPayload \u003d {\n                title: taskData.title,\n                description: taskData.description,\n                status: taskData.status,\n                assignee_id: taskData.assigneeId,\n            };\n\n            const response \u003d await fetch(\u0027/gindoo/api/tasks/create\u0027, {\n                method: \u0027POST\u0027,\n                headers: { \u0027Content-Type\u0027: \u0027application/json\u0027 },\n                body: JSON.stringify(payload), // Send the snake_case payload\n            });\n            // ... (rest of the function is the same)\n        } catch (error) { /* ... */ }\n    };\n\n    const updateTask \u003d async (taskId: string, updates: Partial\u003cTask\u003e) \u003d\u003e {\n        try {\n            // +++ TRANSFORM to the API payload format +++\n            const payload: TaskApiPayload \u003d {\n                title: updates.title,\n                description: updates.description,\n                status: updates.status,\n                assignee_id: updates.assigneeId,\n            };\n\n            const response \u003d await fetch(`/gindoo/api/tasks/${taskId}`, {\n                method: \u0027PUT\u0027,\n                headers: { \u0027Content-Type\u0027: \u0027application/json\u0027 },\n                body: JSON.stringify(payload), // Send the snake_case payload\n            });\n            // ... (rest of the function is the same)\n        } catch (error) { /* ... */ }\n    };\n    \n    // ... (deleteTask and the rest of the provider)\n};\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Simplify the Task Modal**\n\nNow that the `DataContext` handles the data transformation, the `TaskModal` component becomes cleaner. It only needs to work with the idiomatic, camelCase `Task` type.\n\n**Action:** Update the `handleSave` function in `web/gindoo/src/components/TaskModal.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/components/TaskModal.tsx\n\n// ... (imports and component function signature)\n\n    const handleSave \u003d async (e: React.FormEvent) \u003d\u003e {\n        e.preventDefault();\n        if (!title) {\n            setError(\u0027Title is a required field.\u0027);\n            return;\n        }\n        \n        setIsSubmitting(true);\n        setError(null);\n\n        // +++ CORRECTED: Create the data object using the camelCase Task type +++\n        const taskData: Partial\u003cTask\u003e \u003d {\n            title,\n            description,\n            status,\n            assigneeId: assigneeId \u003d\u003d\u003d \u0027unassigned\u0027 ? null : assigneeId,\n        };\n\n        try {\n            if (isEditing) {\n                await updateTask(taskToEdit.id!, taskData);\n            } else {\n                await addTask(taskData);\n            }\n            onClose();\n        } catch (apiError: any) {\n            setError(apiError.message || \u0027An error occurred while saving the task.\u0027);\n        } finally {\n            setIsSubmitting(false);\n        }\n    };\n\n    // ... (rest of the component\u0027s JSX remains the same)\n```\n\nThis corrected implementation resolves the TypeScript error by creating a clear boundary between the frontend\u0027s internal data structures and the backend\u0027s API contract. The modal now correctly passes the camelCased `assigneeId` to the context, and the context correctly transforms it to the snake_cased `assignee_id` before sending it to the server.\n\nThank you again for the precise feedback. We can now confidently proceed to the final step of building the dashboard.",
