      "text": "You have caught another excellent and crucial error. My apologies. This is a classic TypeScript-specific issue, and it demonstrates a flaw in my previous implementation. The error message is precise and points directly to the root cause.\n\nYour analysis is correct. The browser is trying to import a `.ts` file, and even if it could, that file wouldn\u0027t contain a real JavaScript `export` for `User` because `interface` is a type-only construct.\n\n### **The Root Cause: TypeScript Type Erasure**\n\nThe fundamental reason for this error is **TypeScript\u0027s Type Erasure**.\n\n1.  In `web/gindoo/src/types/index.ts`, we define `export interface User { ... }`.\n2.  An `interface` (or a `type` alias) is a compile-time-only construct. It\u0027s used by the TypeScript compiler to check your code for correctness.\n3.  When Vite transpiles your TypeScript code into JavaScript (the only thing browsers understand), it **completely erases** all `interface` and `type` definitions because they have no runtime equivalent in JavaScript.\n4.  As a result, the compiled `types/index.js` file that is served to the browser is effectively **empty**. It contains no code and certainly no `export` statement.\n5.  The `AuthContext.tsx` file, when compiled, still contains an `import` statement trying to get the `User` export from that empty module, which causes the browser to throw the \"does not provide an export named \u0027User\u0027\" error.\n\n### **The Solution: Type-Only Imports**\n\nTo fix this, we must tell TypeScript that we are only importing a *type definition*, not a real JavaScript value. This allows the compiler to use the type for checking but instructs it to erase the entire `import` statement from the final JavaScript output, preventing the runtime error.\n\nWe do this using the `import type` syntax.\n\n---\n\n### **Corrected Implementation: Applying `import type`**\n\nI will now correct every file that imports a type from our `types/index.ts` module.\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/src/contexts/AuthContext.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/contexts/AuthContext.tsx\n\nimport React, { createContext, useState, useContext, useEffect, ReactNode } from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport type { User } from \u0027../types\u0027; // +++ CORRECTED: Use \u0027import type\u0027\n\n// ... (rest of the file is correct)\n```\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/src/contexts/DataContext.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/contexts/DataContext.tsx\n\nimport React, { createContext, useState, useContext, useEffect, ReactNode } from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport type { Task, User, TaskApiPayload } from \u0027../types\u0027; // +++ CORRECTED: Use \u0027import type\u0027\nimport { useAuth } from \u0027./AuthContext\u0027;\n\n// ... (rest of the file is correct)\n```\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/src/components/TaskModal.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/components/TaskModal.tsx\n\nimport React, { useState, useEffect } from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport type { Task } from \u0027../types\u0027; // +++ CORRECTED: Use \u0027import type\u0027\nimport { useData } from \u0027../contexts/DataContext\u0027;\n\n// ... (rest of the file is correct)\n```\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/src/components/KanbanColumn.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/components/KanbanColumn.tsx\n\nimport React from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport type { Task } from \u0027../types\u0027; // +++ CORRECTED: Use \u0027import type\u0027\nimport { TaskCard } from \u0027./TaskCard\u0027;\n// ... (rest of imports)\n\ninterface KanbanColumnProps {\n    status: \u0027todo\u0027 | \u0027in_progress\u0027 | \u0027done\u0027;\n    title: string;\n    tasks: Task[];\n    onCardClick: (task: Task) \u003d\u003e void;\n}\n\n// ... (rest of the file is correct)\n```\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/src/components/TaskCard.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/components/TaskCard.tsx\n\nimport React from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport type { Task } from \u0027../types\u0027; // +++ CORRECTED: Use \u0027import type\u0027\nimport { useSortable } from \u0027@dnd-kit/sortable\u0027;\n// ... (rest of imports)\n\ninterface TaskCardProps {\n    task: Task;\n    onClick: (task: Task) \u003d\u003e void;\n}\n\n// ... (rest of the file is correct)\n```\n\n**Action:** Update the file `web/gindoo/src/components/UserAvatar.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/components/UserAvatar.tsx\n\nimport React from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport type { User } from \u0027../types\u0027; // +++ CORRECTED: Use \u0027import type\u0027\n\ninterface UserAvatarProps {\n    user?: Partial\u003cUser\u003e | null;\n}\n\n// ... (rest of the file is correct)\n```\n\n---\n\nThese changes correctly separate the compile-time type information from the runtime JavaScript modules. The browser will no longer attempt to fetch an empty file, and the application will load without the syntax error.\n\nThank you once again for your diligence in testing and reporting this. This correction is vital for a stable application. We can now proceed with confidence.",
