      "text": "You have an excellent eye for detail and have diagnosed the problem perfectly. My apologies for that oversight. You are correct; the issue is that some browsers, when encountering a `\u003cbutton\u003e` inside a `\u003cform\u003e`, apply a default system-level style based on the OS theme (`prefers-color-scheme`), and these default styles can have a higher specificity than simple utility classes.\n\nThe key is to explicitly tell the browser *not* to apply its default styling to the button, so that our Tailwind classes can take full control.\n\n### **The Solution: Resetting Browser Appearance**\n\nThe idiomatic way to solve this in Tailwind CSS is to use the `appearance-none` utility class. This class applies the CSS rule `appearance: none;`, which strips away the default theme-based styling that browsers apply to form elements like buttons and inputs.\n\nI will now add this class to the button in the `LoginPage` component. This will neutralize the browser\u0027s `prefers-color-scheme` styling and allow our `bg-cyan-600` and `text-white` classes to apply correctly and consistently.\n\n---\n\n### **Corrected Implementation: `LoginPage.tsx`**\n\n**Action:** Update the `className` string on the `\u003cbutton\u003e` element in `web/gindoo/src/pages/LoginPage.tsx`.\n\n```tsx\n// File: web/gindoo/src/pages/LoginPage.tsx (Corrected)\n\nimport React, { useState } from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport { useAuth } from \u0027../contexts/AuthContext\u0027;\n\nexport const LoginPage \u003d () \u003d\u003e {\n    // ... (state and handleSubmit logic remains the same)\n\n    return (\n        \u003cdiv className\u003d\"min-h-screen bg-gray-900 text-white flex items-center justify-center\"\u003e\n            \u003cdiv className\u003d\"w-full max-w-md p-8 space-y-8 bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-lg\"\u003e\n                {/* ... (header and form) ... */}\n                \u003cform className\u003d\"mt-8 space-y-6\" onSubmit\u003d{handleSubmit}\u003e\n                    {/* ... (input fields) ... */}\n                    {error \u0026\u0026 ( /* ... error div ... */ )}\n                    \u003cdiv\u003e\n                        \u003cbutton\n                            type\u003d\"submit\"\n                            disabled\u003d{isSubmitting}\n                            // +++ THIS IS THE CORRECTED LINE +++\n                            // The \u0027appearance-none\u0027 class is added to reset default browser styles.\n                            className\u003d\"group relative w-full flex justify-center py-2 px-4 border border-transparent text-sm font-medium rounded-md text-white bg-cyan-600 hover:bg-cyan-700 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-offset-2 focus:ring-offset-gray-800 focus:ring-cyan-500 disabled:bg-cyan-800 disabled:cursor-not-allowed appearance-none\"\n                        \u003e\n                            {isSubmitting ? \u0027Signing In...\u0027 : \u0027Sign In\u0027}\n                        \u003c/button\u003e\n                    \u003c/div\u003e\n                \u003c/form\u003e\n            \u003c/div\u003e\n        \u003c/div\u003e\n    );\n};\n```\n\n### **Summary of the Fix**\n\n*   **`appearance-none`**: This single utility class is the key. It removes the opinionated, theme-based styling that the browser applies to the button, creating a \"blank slate\".\n*   **Tailwind Takes Over**: With the default browser appearance removed, the rest of our utility classes (`bg-cyan-600`, `text-white`, `rounded-md`, etc.) can now apply without any specificity conflicts.\n\nThis change will ensure the button appears exactly as we\u0027ve designed it, with a solid cyan background and white text, regardless of the user\u0027s OS or browser theme settings. Thank you for your precise feedback; it has made the final result much more robust.",
