      "text": "You are absolutely right to focus on the functional aspect. A visible button that doesn\u0027t work is useless, and an invisible button is a complete blocker. Let\u0027s get you logged in.\n\nThe core of the problem is that to log in, a user account must first exist in the database. Since we haven\u0027t built the registration UI yet, we need to create a user directly via the API.\n\nHere is the step-by-step process to create a user and log in to the application.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Create a User Account**\n\nWe will use a `cURL` command from your terminal to send a `POST` request directly to the registration endpoint we already built. This will create the first user in your SQLite database.\n\n**Action:** Open a new terminal (do not stop the running `npm run dev` process) and execute the following command:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:7070/gindoo/api/auth/register \\\n-H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n-d \u0027{\"name\": \"Test User\", \"email\": \"test@example.com\", \"password\": \"password123\"}\u0027\n```\n\n**Expected Output:**\nAfter running the command, you should see a JSON response in your terminal confirming that the user was created successfully:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"some-generated-uuid\",\n  \"name\": \"Test User\",\n  \"email\": \"test@example.com\",\n  \"avatar_url\": null\n}\n```\nYou now have an account in the database with the email `test@example.com` and the password `password123`.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Log In Through the UI**\n\nNow you can use these credentials to log in through the application\u0027s user interface.\n\n**Action:**\n1.  Go back to the Gindoo login page in your browser (`http://localhost:7070/gindoo`).\n2.  Enter the following credentials into the form fields:\n    *   **Email address:** `test@example.com`\n    *   **Password:** `password123`\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Submit the Form**\n\nSince the \"Sign In\" button is currently invisible due to the CSS issue, you can submit the form by **pressing the `Enter` key** while your cursor is in the password field.\n\nThis will trigger the form submission, call our `login` API, and upon successful authentication, the application will transition you from the `LoginPage` to the placeholder `KanbanPage`. You should see the screen change to a dark background with the heading \"Kanban Board\".\n\nThis confirms that the entire authentication flow—from frontend to backend, including JWT creation and cookie handling—is working correctly. We can now confidently proceed with building out the rest of the UI, including a proper registration page and fixing the button style.",
