      "text": "Thank you for providing this detailed user flow. It is perfectly clear and provides an excellent roadmap for the application\u0027s final functionality. This is a much more refined and complete vision than the previous iteration.\n\nI have fully assimilated the new user flow and have no issues with the logic. The separation of roles and the navigation paths are well-defined and follow standard application design patterns.\n\nBefore we proceed, I have one suggestion and a few clarifying questions to ensure the implementation is as robust as possible.\n\n### **Suggestion: A Unified Navigation Component**\n\nTo ensure maximum consistency and maintainability, I suggest we create a single, intelligent `Navbar` component. This component would contain *all* possible navigation links (`Kanban`, `Dashboard`, `Users`, `Profile`). Inside this component, we would use the `useAuth()` hook to get the current user\u0027s role and conditionally render the links based on the logic you provided.\n\n**Benefits:**\n*   **Single Source of Truth:** All navigation logic is in one file (`components/Navbar.tsx`), making it very easy to add, remove, or change links in the future without editing multiple pages.\n*   **Consistency:** We guarantee that the header looks and behaves identically across every page that uses the `MainLayout`.\n\nThe `MainLayout` would then simply render this `Navbar` component, and the individual pages (`KanbanPage`, `DashboardPage`, `AdminUsersPage`) would contain no navigation logic at all.\n\n### **Clarifying Questions**\n\nTo ensure the backend and database are perfectly aligned with this user flow, I have a few questions:\n\n1.  **\"Profile\" Page Functionality:** You mentioned a \"Profile\" page where a normal user can change their password. This will require a new backend API endpoint.\n    *   **Proposed Endpoint:** `PUT /api/users/me/password`\n    *   **Payload:** `{ \"currentPassword\": \"...\", \"newPassword\": \"...\" }`\n    *   **Logic:** The backend script would verify the `currentPassword` against the hash in the database before updating it with the new, hashed password.\n    *   **Question:** Does this proposed endpoint and logic for the password change meet your requirements?\n\n2.  **Task Creation (Assignee):** When a normal (non-admin) user creates a new task, should they be able to assign it to any other user, or should it default to being assigned to themselves? Allowing them to assign to anyone is fine, but it\u0027s a business rule worth confirming.\n\n3.  **Task Modification/Deletion:** Should a normal user be able to edit or delete a task that is assigned to another user, or only their own tasks? My suggestion would be to restrict this to only their own tasks, while an admin could edit/delete any task.\n\nAnswering these questions will ensure that the security and business logic I build into the API endpoints and the UI components are precisely what you envision for the application.\n\nOnce we\u0027ve clarified these points, I am ready to begin implementing this complete user flow.",
