      "text": "**Addressing Database Errors**\n\nI\u0027ve isolated the user\u0027s issue: a \"column not found\" error related to the \u0027role\u0027 column in their \u0027Users\u0027 table. My initial focus is pinpointing the exact SQL statement causing the problem and then confirming the database schema. Next, I will delve into the user\u0027s setup for context, and offer solutions by the end of today.\n\n\n**Confirming the Root Cause**\n\nI\u0027ve determined the issue. The user\u0027s `gindoo.db` likely predates the corrected schema, preventing `CREATE TABLE` from updating the table structure. This causes the seeding script to fail because it references the non-existent `role` column. The solution involves instructing the user to drop the existing `Users` table and re-run the schema migration.\n\n\n**Proposing a Clear Solution**\n\nI\u0027ve determined the cause and have moved on to crafting a straightforward user-facing resolution. The user\u0027s issue stems from a mismatch between their database schema and the expected table structure, triggered during server startup. To resolve this, I intend to provide concise steps: halt the server, direct the user to remove the existing database file, and then instruct them to restart the server. This simple approach will realign the database with the updated schema, ensuring the application functions correctly.\n\n\n**Devising the Implementation**\n\nI\u0027m solidifying the user-facing instructions. The aim is to eliminate any ambiguity. To make the steps even clearer, I\u0027ll include screenshots showing exactly where the database file is located. Also, I\u0027ll offer a brief explanation of how the schema and seed scripts work together, reinforcing the user\u0027s understanding of the process. This will ensure the solution is both effective and educational.\n\n\n**Refining User Instructions**\n\nI\u0027m focused on clarity and thoroughness in the user instructions. I\u0027ll start by acknowledging the error, then explain its cause in simple terms: the outdated database schema. The core instruction is to delete the `gindoo.db` file, which I\u0027ll provide a specific file path for. I will also provide the corrected schema script again, and describe the expected outcome, reinforcing the process.\n\n\n",
