      "text": "You are absolutely right. My apologies. I made an error in the Tailwind CSS class for the modal backdrop. You are correct that `bg-black` creates a solid black background, overriding any opacity settings.\n\nTo achieve the correct semi-transparent gray effect, I should have used Tailwind\u0027s color opacity modifier syntax.\n\n### **The Corrected Code**\n\nThe fix is a one-line change in the `TaskModal.tsx` component.\n\n**Action:** Update the outermost `div` in `web/gindoo/src/components/TaskModal.tsx`.\n\n**Current Incorrect Code:**\n```tsx\n\u003cdiv className\u003d\"fixed inset-0 bg-black bg-opacity-75 flex items-center justify-center z-50 p-4\" onClick\u003d{onClose}\u003e\n```\n\n**Corrected Code:**\n```tsx\n\u003cdiv className\u003d\"fixed inset-0 bg-gray-900/75 flex items-center justify-center z-50 p-4\" onClick\u003d{onClose}\u003e\n```\n\n### **Explanation of the Fix**\n\n*   **`bg-black`**: This class sets `background-color: rgb(0 0 0)`.\n*   **`bg-opacity-75`**: This class sets `--tw-bg-opacity: 0.75`, which works with colors defined with opacity variables, but `bg-black` does not use one.\n*   **`bg-gray-900/75`**: This is the modern and correct Tailwind CSS syntax. It directly applies `background-color: rgb(17 24 39 / 0.75)`, giving us a dark slate gray color (`gray-900`) with 75% opacity, resulting in the exact translucent effect you described.\n\nThank you for the precise correction. With this change, the modal\u0027s backdrop will now render correctly.",
