      "text": "**Breaking Down User Requests**\n\nI\u0027m starting by dissecting the user\u0027s feedback. I\u0027m aiming to identify three specific, actionable items within their request, to facilitate a clear response. This initial breakdown will form the foundation for a detailed answer.\n\n\n**Defining Actionable Items**\n\nI\u0027ve successfully identified the three key, actionable items from the user\u0027s feedback: the user filter style, the Kanban column height/scrolling issue, and the responsive header requirement. This dissection allows me to focus on creating solutions for each concern. The first is a straightforward style fix, while the other two involve deeper layout and state management considerations.\n\n\n**Strategizing Layout Solutions**\n\nI\u0027m now formulating strategies for the Kanban column layout. I\u0027ve realized the `MainLayout` uses `min-h-screen`, and the `KanbanPage` needs to leverage that. Making the `main` element a `flex flex-col` seems key to distributing the height properly. On desktop, I\u0027ll aim for internal scrolling within columns using flexbox. On mobile, the default stacking behavior will work.\n\n\n**Developing Layout Strategies**\n\nI\u0027m now devising the precise layout adjustments for the Kanban columns.  I\u0027ve decided that a `flex flex-col` approach on the Kanban page is the most robust way to ensure that the main element fills the screen height, while allowing internal scrolling within columns on desktop. On mobile, the existing stacking behavior will remain. I\u0027ll need to adjust the `overflow-y` property of each column\u0027s task list as well.\n\n\n**Finalizing Kanban Layout**\n\nThe Kanban column layout is now fully strategized. I\u0027ll make the `KanbanPage`\u0027s main element a `flex flex-col` to take up the full screen height.  Each column within the grid will be a flex container with `overflow-y-auto` on the task list.  This allows internal scrolling on desktop while keeping the default stacking behavior on mobile.\n\n\n**Implementing Style \u0026 Layout**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing the CSS fixes for the user filter and Kanban column layout.  I\u0027m replacing dark theme classes with light theme equivalents in `UserFilter.tsx`. For the Kanban board, I\u0027ve transformed the `KanbanPage`\u0027s main element into a `flex flex-col` container. I\u0027ve also added `overflow-y-auto` to the task list within each column, in `KanbanColumn.tsx`. The default stacking behavior on mobile remains intact, while desktop columns now scroll internally.\n\n\n**Optimizing Kanban Board**\n\nI\u0027ve determined that the `KanbanPage`\u0027s main element should be a `flex flex-col` container that fully occupies the screen\u0027s height. Each `KanbanColumn` grid child will also be a flex container. The scrollable content will be within the column\u0027s task list element. This structure allows for internal scrolling on desktop, and standard stacking on mobile.\n\n\n",
