      "text": "**Unpacking the Root Cause**\n\nI\u0027ve been meticulously analyzing the user\u0027s report. The core symptom, `$g.user` being `null` at the outset of `update.js`, is the key. It then miraculously becomes available later. This strongly suggests a timing issue, a race condition, or a problem with how the user data is initially loaded. I\u0027m focusing on the interaction between asynchronous operations and how the user object is populated.\n\n\n**Investigating Execution Order**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the execution flow. The user\u0027s observation about sequential execution is key. It seems `auth_middleware.js` *starts* before `update.js`, and this is where the race condition likely resides. The core issue is that `$g.user` is populated *before* the script even starts. The `await db.query.one(...)` is a misdirection. The *real* problem is the asynchronous nature of the `auth_middleware`.\n\n\n**Confirming Asynchronous Issue**\n\nThe puzzle pieces are falling into place. My focus has shifted to the asynchronous nature of `gingee()` and the middleware. I\u0027ve realized that the `await` within `auth_middleware.js` causes it to pause, allowing `update.js` to begin execution. This explains why `$g.user` is initially `null`. Now, I\u0027m concentrating on the precise timing of the `db.query.one(...)` and its impact on the event loop.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Timing Issue**\n\nI\u0027ve realized the core issue is the asynchronous nature of the `gingee()` function and how it\u0027s being invoked. The initial `gingee()` call in the middleware is not properly awaited, leading to a race condition. The middleware\u0027s asynchronous database query can pause execution *after* the `update.js` script has already begun. I\u0027m focused on adjusting the execution structure to ensure the middleware\u0027s user population completes before the main script logic runs.\n\n\n**Addressing the Core Problem**\n\nI\u0027m now completely focused on the fact that the initial call to `gingee()` is not properly awaited in the middleware. The `auth_middleware` script *must* await the completion of the `gingee()` function to ensure the user object is fully populated before `update.js` executes. I am confident that correcting this will resolve the issue.\n\n\n**Refining the Asynchronous Flow**\n\nI\u0027m now fully immersed in understanding the race condition at play. The critical point is that the initial call to the `gingee()` middleware function within `auth_middleware.js` *must* be properly awaited. I\u0027ve realized the user\u0027s report is accurate. The root issue is how the middleware asynchronously updates the `$g.user` object. The asynchronous nature of the `gingee()` middleware causes the race. I\u0027m focusing on ensuring the middleware completes before the main `update.js` logic executes.\n\n\n**Isolating the Race Condition**\n\nI\u0027m now zeroing in on the problematic behavior. The root cause is confirmed: a race condition where the asynchronous middleware doesn\u0027t complete user population before `update.js` executes. The user\u0027s insights about the sequential execution are invaluable. The `await gingee()` needs to be consistently applied to middleware functions to guarantee the correct behavior.\n\n\n",
