      "text": "You have found the root of the problem. My deepest apologies. This is a subtle but extremely critical bug in the code I have been generating, and your analysis is absolutely, 100% correct.\n\n**There is a race condition.**\n\nYou are right that the middleware and the server script *should* be executed sequentially. The Gingee engine is designed to do this. However, I have made a repeated mistake in how I\u0027ve written the middleware script that violates this sequential promise.\n\n### **The Root Cause: Fire-and-Forget `gingee()` Call**\n\nLet\u0027s look at the `auth_middleware.js` script I provided:\n\n```javascript\n// The Flawed Pattern I Provided:\nmodule.exports \u003d async function() {\n    // This calls gingee but DOES NOT wait for its inner async handler to complete.\n    gingee(async ($g) \u003d\u003e { \n        // ...\n        await db.query.one(...); // \u003c--- The event loop pauses HERE...\n        $g.user \u003d user;          // \u003c--- ...and this line runs LATER.\n    });\n    // The module.exports function returns IMMEDIATELY after starting the gingee() call.\n};\n```\n\nHere is the race condition you correctly identified:\n\n1.  The Gingee engine calls the `auth_middleware.js` script.\n2.  The script\u0027s main function immediately calls `gingee(...)` but does **not** `await` it. This is a \"fire-and-forget\" operation.\n3.  The `module.exports` function finishes instantly. From the engine\u0027s perspective, the middleware script is \"done\".\n4.  The engine immediately proceeds to execute the next script in the chain: `api/tasks/update.js`.\n5.  `update.js` starts running. At the same time, the `auth_middleware`\u0027s inner handler is waiting for its `await db.query.one(...)` to finish.\n6.  `update.js` checks for `$g.user`. At this moment, it is still `null` because the middleware\u0027s database call has not completed yet.\n7.  This is why you see the error. If you move the check past an `await` in `update.js`, you are simply giving the middleware\u0027s database call enough time to finish and populate `$g.user`.\n\n### **The Definitive Solution: `await gingee()`**\n\nTo enforce sequential execution and guarantee that the middleware completes *before* the main script runs, we must `await` the `gingee()` call itself within the exported function.\n\nI will now correct this fundamental flaw in all relevant scripts.\n\n---\n\n**Action 1: Correct the `auth_middleware.js`**\n\nThis is the most critical fix.\n\n**File:** `web/gindoo/box/auth_middleware.js` (Corrected)\n```javascript\nmodule.exports \u003d async function() {\n    // By adding \u0027await\u0027 here, we force the entire outer function\n    // to wait until the inner async handler has fully completed.\n    await gingee(async ($g) \u003d\u003e {\n        const auth \u003d require(\u0027auth\u0027);\n        const db \u003d require(\u0027db\u0027);\n        const DB_NAME \u003d \u0027main_db\u0027;\n\n        $g.user \u003d null;\n\n        const token \u003d $g.request.cookies.gindoo_session;\n        if (token) {\n            const payload \u003d auth.jwt.verify(token);\n            if (payload \u0026\u0026 payload.userId) {\n                const user \u003d await db.query.one(\n                    DB_NAME, \n                    \u0027SELECT id, name, email, avatar_url, role FROM \"Users\" WHERE \"id\" \u003d $1\u0027, \n                    [payload.userId]\n                );\n                if (user) {\n                    $g.user \u003d user;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    });\n};\n```\n\n**Action 2: Apply the Same Pattern to All Other Backend Scripts for Consistency and Safety**\n\nWhile the race condition is most critical in the middleware, this explicit `await` is the correct pattern for all server scripts. I will update the other files to match.\n\n**File:** `web/gindoo/box/api/tasks/update.js` (Corrected)\n```javascript\nmodule.exports \u003d async function() {\n    // Add the await here as well.\n    await gingee(async ($g) \u003d\u003e {\n        const db \u003d require(\u0027db\u0027);\n        const DB_NAME \u003d \u0027main_db\u0027;\n\n        if (!$g.user) {\n            return $g.response.send({ error: \u0027Unauthorized.\u0027 }, 401);\n        }\n        // ... rest of the function\n    });\n};\n```\n\nThis same `await gingee(...)` pattern will be applied to **all other API scripts** (`login.js`, `register.js`, `delete.js`, etc.) to ensure robust, predictable, and sequential execution in all cases.\n\nMy deepest apologies for this repeated and fundamental error. Your debugging was invaluable in identifying this race condition. With this correction, the sequential execution guarantee of the middleware is restored, `$g.user` will be reliably available in all protected endpoints, and the `TypeError` will be resolved.",
