      "text": "Excellent. This is a great step to make Gindoo a compelling and immediately usable sample application. Seeding the database with realistic data is crucial for demonstrating the app\u0027s features right out of the box.\n\nI have a clear understanding of your requirements:\n*   **Users:** A total of 4 users. One will be the existing administrator, and we will add 3 new non-admin users.\n*   **Password:** All 3 new users will have the password `changepwd123`.\n*   **Tasks:** 10 new tasks will be created.\n*   **Assignment:** These 10 tasks will be distributed among the 3 new non-admin users.\n*   **Status:** The tasks will be in various states (`todo`, `in_progress`, `done`).\n*   **Theme:** The data should feel like it belongs to a software scrum team.\n\nBefore I proceed with the implementation, I have a few clarifying questions to ensure the final result is exactly what you envision.\n\n---\n\n### **Doubts Before Implementation**\n\n1.  **Seeding Strategy (Idempotency):** Startup scripts can run every time the server restarts. To prevent creating duplicate users and tasks on every launch, the script needs to be \"idempotent\" (meaning it can run multiple times without changing the result after the first run). My proposed strategy is: **The script will only add the 3 sample users and 10 sample tasks if it detects that only 1 user (the admin) currently exists in the database.** This makes the seeding a one-time operation for a fresh installation. Is this the correct approach?\n\n2.  **Task Distribution:** How would you like the 10 tasks distributed among the 3 non-admin users? I propose the following realistic breakdown to make the board look active:\n    *   **User 1 (e.g., Alice - Frontend Dev):** 4 tasks\n    *   **User 2 (e.g., Bob - Backend Dev):** 3 tasks\n    *   **User 3 (e.g., Charlie - QA/UX):** 3 tasks\n    Does this distribution seem appropriate?\n\n3.  **Task Status Distribution:** How should the statuses of the 10 tasks be distributed? To showcase a project in progress, I propose:\n    *   **4 tasks** in \"To Do\"\n    *   **3 tasks** in \"In Progress\"\n    *   **3 tasks** in \"Done\"\n    Does this reflect a typical project state you\u0027d like to showcase?\n\n4.  **Implementation File:** I will create a new startup script named **`setup/03_seed_sample_data.js`** for this purpose. This keeps the essential admin creation (`02_seed_admin.js`) separate from the optional sample data, which a developer might want to disable later. Is this approach acceptable?\n\n---\n\nOnce these details are confirmed, I will proceed with writing the `03_seed_sample_data.js` script and updating the `app.json` to include it in the startup sequence. I am ready to implement as soon as you give the go-ahead.",
