      "text": "Excellent. This is the final and most critical phase of any project: moving from a development environment to a production-ready, distributable package. Gingee is specifically designed to make this process simple, secure, and repeatable.\n\nHere is the complete, step-by-step guide to prepare the Gindoo application for production testing and distribution.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 1: Build the React Frontend for Production**\n\nFirst, we need to compile our React/TypeScript source code into optimized, static HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files that can be served directly.\n\n**Action:**\n1.  Navigate into the Gindoo application\u0027s directory:\n    ```bash\n    cd web/gindoo\n    ```\n2.  Run the standard build script defined in the frontend `package.json`:\n    ```bash\n    npm run build\n    ```\n\n**Result:**\nThis command will invoke Vite, which will build the application and create a `dist` folder inside `web/gindoo/`. This folder contains the highly optimized assets that will be served to users in production.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 2: Switch the Application to Production Mode**\n\nNext, we must instruct the Gingee engine to stop using the development proxy and start serving the static files from our new `dist` folder.\n\n**Action:**\nUpdate the `app.json` file to change the `mode` from \"development\" to \"production\".\n\n**File:** `web/gindoo/box/app.json`\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Gindoo\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"type\": \"SPA\",\n  \"mode\": \"production\", // \u003c-- CHANGE THIS LINE\n  \"spa\": {\n    \"enabled\": true,\n    // dev_server_proxy is now ignored by the engine in production mode\n    \"dev_server_proxy\": \"http://localhost:5173\", \n    \"build_path\": \"../dist\",\n    \"fallback_path\": \"index.html\"\n  },\n  // ... rest of the file\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Step 3: Create the Distribution Manifests**\n\nTo create a proper, secure Gingee package (`.gin` file), we must define two manifests in the `box` folder.\n\n**1. Permissions Manifest (`pmft.json`)**\nThis file declares the permissions Gindoo requires. When another administrator installs our app, the CLI will use this file to ask for their consent.\n\n**Action:** Create the file `web/gindoo/box/pmft.json`.\n```json\n{\n  \"permissions\": {\n    \"mandatory\": [\n      \"db\",\n      \"crypto\",\n      \"uuid\",\n      \"utils\",\n      \"auth\",\n      \"chart\"\n    ],\n    \"optional\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Package Contents Manifest (`.gpkg`)**\nThis file tells the packager which files to include in the final `.gin` archive. We must include the `box` (backend) and `dist` (frontend) folders, while excluding all development-only files and folders.\n\n**Action:** Create the file `web/gindoo/box/.gpkg`.\n```json\n{\n  \"include\": [\n    \"box/**/*\",\n    \"dist/**/*\"\n  ],\n  \"exclude\": [\n    \"src\",\n    \"node_modules\",\n    \".gitignore\",\n    \"vite.config.js\",\n    \"tailwind.config.js\",\n    \"postcss.config.js\",\n    \"package.json\",\n    \"package-lock.json\",\n    \"tsconfig.json\",\n    \"tsconfig.node.json\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Step 4: Package the Application**\n\nWith the frontend built and the manifests in place, we can now create the distributable `.gin` package using the CLI.\n\n**Action:**\n1.  Ensure your local Gingee server is running (`npm run start` or `npm run dev` from the project root).\n2.  From the project root, run the package command:\n    ```bash\n    gingee-cli package-app --appName gindoo\n    ```\n\n**Result:**\nThe CLI will connect to your local server, read the Gindoo application files according to the `.gpkg` rules, and generate a versioned package file in your project root, for example: `gindoo-v1.0.0.gin`.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 5: Test Locally in Production Mode**\n\nBefore deploying elsewhere, you can test the production setup on your local machine.\n\n**Action:**\n1.  Stop your development server (`Ctrl+C`).\n2.  Start the server using the standard command, which does not use `nodemon`:\n    ```bash\n    npm start\n    ```\n\nNow, when you navigate to `http://localhost:7070/gindoo`, the Gingee server will be serving the static files directly from the `web/gindoo/dist` directory and handling the SPA fallback routing, exactly as it would on a real production server.\n\n---\n\n### **Step 6: Distribute and Deploy**\n\nThe `gindoo-v1.0.0.gin` file is now a self-contained, portable application package. You can deploy it to any other machine running a Gingee server.\n\n**To deploy on a new server:**\n1.  Copy the `gindoo-v1.0.0.gin` file to the new server.\n2.  Log in to the new server\u0027s Glade instance:\n    ```bash\n    gingee-cli login --serverUrl http://\u003cnew-server-ip\u003e:7070\n    ```\n3.  Run the interactive installation command:\n    ```bash\n    gingee-cli install-app --serverUrl http://\u003cnew-server-ip\u003e:7070 --appName gindoo --ginPath ./gindoo-v1.0.0.gin\n    ```\n\nThe CLI will guide you through the permission consent and database configuration on the new server, completing the deployment.\n\nThe Gindoo application is now fully prepared for distribution and production use.",
