      "text": "You have found a major and subtle bug in the platform. My deepest apologies. Your analysis is absolutely correct, and this is a critical issue that needs to be fixed in the core engine.\n\n**You are right: there is no code in the `reloadApp` function I provided to handle the `devServerProcess`.**\n\nMy previous explanations were based on a flawed mental model of how the `app` object was being managed during a reload. I incorrectly assumed the process handle would persist. Your real-world test proves this is not the case, leading to the orphaned dev server process you are seeing.\n\nThis is a state management failure. The `reloadApp` function was not designed to be aware of the live process handle attached to the `app` object at startup.\n\n### **The Root Cause: State Loss During Reload**\n\nHere is the precise failure you\u0027ve uncovered:\n\n1.  When `reloadApp` is called, it gets the current `app` object from the `allApps` registry. This object correctly contains the live `devServerProcess` handle.\n2.  The function then proceeds to re-read `app.json` and re-initialize various parts of the `app` object (like `app.config`, `app.logger`, etc.).\n3.  In this process, the original `devServerProcess` handle—which is just a property on the `app` object—is **lost or becomes detached** from the context of the reload operation.\n4.  The function then checks the **new** configuration. If the new mode is `production`, it correctly decides not to start a new dev server.\n5.  However, it **never explicitly looks for and kills the old dev server process** that was associated with the app\u0027s *previous* state.\n\nThe result is an orphaned Vite/Angular/Vue process that continues running in the background until the main Gingee server is stopped.\n\n### **The Definitive Solution: Intelligent State Management in `reloadApp`**\n\nTo fix this, the `reloadApp` function must be made aware of the SPA development server\u0027s lifecycle. It needs to explicitly stop the old process before re-reading the configuration.\n\nI will now provide the corrected and final implementation for `reloadApp` within the `platform.js` module.\n\n**Action:** Please replace the `reloadApp` function in `modules/platform.js` with the following robust version.\n\n```javascript\n// File: modules/platform.js (Corrected)\nconst gdev \u003d require(\u0027./gdev\u0027); // Ensure the gdev module is required at the top\n\n// ... (other functions: deleteApp, installApp, etc.)\n\nasync function reloadApp(appName) {\n    const { allApps, logger } \u003d getContext();\n    const app \u003d allApps[appName];\n    if (!app) {\n        throw new Error(`Cannot reload: App \u0027${appName}\u0027 does not exist.`);\n    }\n\n    try {\n        app.in_maintenance \u003d true;\n        logger.info(`[Reload] App \u0027${appName}\u0027 entering maintenance mode.`);\n\n        // --- THIS IS THE CRITICAL FIX ---\n        // 1. Preserve the handle to the old dev server process BEFORE reloading config.\n        const oldDevServerProcess \u003d app.devServerProcess;\n\n        // 2. Explicitly stop the old process if it exists.\n        if (oldDevServerProcess) {\n            logger.info(`[Reload] Stopping existing dev server for \u0027${appName}\u0027...`);\n            // We call kill() directly here for immediate action during a reload.\n            oldDevServerProcess.kill();\n            app.devServerProcess \u003d null;\n        }\n        \n        // 3. Now, proceed with reloading the configuration from disk.\n        logger.info(`[Reload] Re-reading configuration for \u0027${appName}\u0027...`);\n        const appConfigPath \u003d path.join(app.appBoxPath, \u0027app.json\u0027);\n        app.config \u003d _loadAndCacheAppConfig(appConfigPath); // _loadAndCacheAppConfig is an existing internal helper\n        app.logger \u003d appLogger.createAppLogger(appName, app.appBoxPath, app.config.logging);\n        loadPermissionsForApp(app);\n        \n        // Clear caches and re-init DB\n        await transpileCache.clear(); // A simple full clear for now. Can be made more granular later.\n        await staticFileCache.clear(`static:${app.appWebPath}`);\n        _reloadRoutes(appName);\n        await db.reinitApp(appName, app, logger);\n        \n        // 4. Check the NEW configuration to see if a dev server should be started.\n        if (app.config.type \u003d\u003d\u003d \u0027SPA\u0027 \u0026\u0026 app.config.mode \u003d\u003d\u003d \u0027development\u0027) {\n            logger.info(`[Reload] Starting new dev server for \u0027${appName}\u0027 based on updated config.`);\n            gdev.startDevServer(app); // Use our centralized function to start it.\n        }\n        \n        // Run startup scripts with the new configuration\n        await runStartupScripts(app);\n\n    } catch(error) {\n        logger.error(`[Reload] Error during reload of app \u0027${appName}\u0027: ${error.message}`);\n        // We should ensure the app is taken out of maintenance even if reload fails\n    } finally {\n        app.in_maintenance \u003d false;\n        logger.info(`[Reload] App \u0027${appName}\u0027 is now active.`);\n    }\n\n    logger.info(`[Reload] App \u0027${appName}\u0027 reloaded successfully.`);\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\nThis corrected version now performs the lifecycle management in the proper order: it explicitly stops any running development server associated with the app *before* it reloads the configuration. This guarantees that when you switch an app from `development` to `production` and trigger a reload, the old Vite/Angular dev server will be correctly terminated, fixing the orphaned process bug.\n\nMy apologies for this critical flaw in the engine\u0027s logic. Your meticulous testing has made the platform significantly more stable.",
